%% ABSTRACT-BEGIN
\begin{abstract}
On-scalp optically pumped magnetometers (OPMs) record larger fields than cryogenic magnetoencephalography (MEG), but in a spherical head their signal-to-noise ratio is higher only for sources shallower than a depth set by the sensor-noise ratio. Here, we replaced that ratio with explicit sensor, brain and room noise and compared the << F.g2_squid_channels >>-channel Neuromag system with dense and site-matched OPM arrays by known-topography detectability in an adult and << F.wr_n_smaller_heads_words >> smaller heads (<< F.wr_n_scaled_words >> scaled adults, << F.wr_n_templates_words >> infant templates, << F.wr_n_children_words >> school-aged children). In the adult, with sensor plus brain noise, a dense << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>-fT/√Hz array had << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >> times Neuromag's detectability, a lead not established at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >>~fT/√Hz; the site-matched array had no advantage. With measured noise, Neuromag's detectability was << F.rev_cov_det_combined_corrected_ratio >> times the modeled value, as much as the dense lead, which thus remains a prediction for measured OPM noise to test. Exploratory regional analyses showed the smallest dense lead in cingulate cortex and a site-matched deficit on ventral and polar surfaces. In the fixed adult helmet, the scaled adults and templates gained << F.g3b_templates_scaled_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB over the adult; fitting the helmet to each head at the adult's top-contact gap left << F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_fittedtop_vs_top_range >>~dB. In a confirmatory simulation (idealized detector, modeled noise, fixed adult helmet), the dense array detected spikes << F.cf_band >>~mm deep at << F.cf_all_dense_vs_combined_practical_reduction_pct_range >> lower strength than Neuromag in all << F.wr_n_anatomies_words >> anatomies; exploratory localization showed no difference. Sensor noise, array design and the fit of the adult helmet, more than head size, set the modeled advantage of on-scalp MEG.
\end{abstract}
