\section{Materials and methods}\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Study design and estimands}\label{sec:design}

We compared arrays by known-topography detectability, the output SNR of the matched filter for a source of known field pattern in noise of known covariance:
\begin{equation}
d = \sqrt{\symbf{s}^{\top}\symbf{C}^{-1}\symbf{s}},
\label{eq:d}
\end{equation}
where $\symbf{s}$ is the noise-free field of a << F.g2_focal_nam >>-nAm cortical dipole at the array's channels and $\symbf{C}$ is the array's noise covariance (Section~\ref{sec:noise}), taken as known (the oracle covariance). Two arrays were compared target by target by the paired ratio $d_{\mathrm{OPM}}/d_{\mathrm{Neuromag}}$ or, in decibels, by
\begin{equation}
D = 20\log_{10}\frac{d_{\mathrm{OPM}}}{d_{\mathrm{Neuromag}}},
\label{eq:D}
\end{equation}
where $D$ is positive when the OPM array detects the source better. Neuromag denotes all << F.g2_squid_channels >> channels unless stated otherwise.

For the adult, we report the unweighted median of the paired ratio over all << F.g2_n_targets >> targets, including the << F.g2_medialwall_n >> on the medial wall; without them, the dense array's ratio is << F.wr_g2_dense_ib_ratio_no_medial_wall >>. The smaller heads differ in cortical area, so for them we report $D$ as an area-weighted median over cortical targets, medial wall excluded, and its change from the adult, $\Delta = D_{\mathrm{head}} - D_{\mathrm{adult}}$, with the adult placed by the same helmet rule. Table~\ref{tab:estimands} defines these and the other estimands of the Results; Table~S2 lists all of them.

