\subsection{Head models}\label{sec:heads}

The adult head was the MNE sample subject \citep{gramfort2013}, with FreeSurfer surfaces and atlas labels \citep{desikan2006,fischl2012}. Targets were the vertices of an oct-6 source space on the white-matter surface lying at least << F.g2_source_skull_clearance_mm >>~mm inside the inner skull, each a << F.g2_focal_nam >>-nAm dipole normal to the cortex, and a target's depth was its distance to the nearest point of the MRI scalp. A three-layer boundary-element model (BEM; \citealp{hamalainen1989}) with conductivities of << F.g2_conductivity >>~S/m had its outer surface on the MRI scalp.

The << F.wr_n_smaller_heads_words >> smaller heads fell into three classes (Table~\ref{tab:anatomies}). The << F.wr_n_scaled_words >> scaled adults were copies of the adult scaled to school-age size and to the head circumference of the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template. The << F.wr_n_templates_words >> infant templates, at << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>, << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >> and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >> months, were averages of many MRIs \citep{oreilly2021,richards2016}. The << F.wr_n_children_words >> typically developing school-aged children, A, B and C (<< F.g3b_children_age_range >> years), came from OpenNeuro dataset ds005234 \citep{fadeev2024,fadeev2025}. The << F.wr_n_anatomies_words >> anatomies are therefore not independent anatomical replications, and all of them share the noise calibration, the adult's conductivities and the processing, so agreement across them does not test those assumptions.

The children's anatomy needed three further steps (Section~S4). First, the dataset's BEM surfaces placed the inner skull a median of << F.g3b_children_watershed_inner_skull_depth_p50_range >>~mm below the scalp over the upper head, implausibly close. We moved it inward toward << F.g3b_cfg_child_skull_depth >>~mm below the scalp wherever it lay shallower, keeping every inner-skull vertex at least << F.g3b_cfg_child_skull_min_cortex >>~mm from the white-surface vertices, so that over the upper head it lay a median of << F.g3b_children_modelled_inner_skull_depth_p50_range >>~mm below the scalp (adult, << F.g3b_adult_inner_skull_depth_p50 >>~mm). Between vertices, the inner skull came within << F.g3b_children_white_to_inner_skull_closest_range >>~mm of the white surface, and in child B the outer skull came within << F.g3b_child_b_outer_skull_to_head_thinnest_2dp >>~mm of the scalp. A single-compartment BEM, which needs no skull, changed the difference between each smaller head's median $D$ and the adult's by << F.meth_bem1_change_db_range >>~dB.

Second, no fiducials come with these data. We transferred them from the adult by a similarity transform fitted between the adult's and the child's white surfaces (trimmed iterative closest points) and moved each to the nearest scalp vertex. Applied to the infant templates, this method reproduced their own fiducials with errors of << F.g3b_templates_fid_transfer_range >>~mm and their head frames with rotations of << F.g3b_templates_fid_transfer_rotation_range >>°. Third, because the children's white surfaces came within << F.rev_qc_children_white_scalp_min_mm_range >>~mm of the scalp, we checked the surfaces against their T1-weighted MRIs (Fig.~S6). The FreeSurfer scalp lay about as far inside the T1 head boundary in the children (median << F.wr_qc_children_offset_inside_otsu_mm_range >>~mm) as in the adult (<< F.wr_qc_adult_offset_inside_otsu_mm >>~mm), and the white surfaces were registered to the T1 images. Relative to these images, the shallow cortex is therefore not a scalp-placement or registration error.

The check cannot verify the white surface within a few millimeters of the scalp or the transferred fiducials. Nor can it detect a head boundary that the T1 images themselves place too deep, which could explain both the shallow cortex and the inner skull lying so close to the scalp. The count of shallow targets also depends on the scalp definition: measured to the MRI head boundary, child B has << F.wr_qc_child_b_targets_lt10mm_mri_n >> rather than << F.g3b_child_b_shallow_targets_lt10mm >> targets less than 10~mm deep. The source rule left out << F.wr_excl_children_pct_range >> of the children's cortex (adult, << F.wr_excl_adult_pct >>), including << F.wr_excl_children_lt8mm_pct_range >> of their cortex within 8~mm of the scalp. We therefore treat the children's results as provisional geometric examples rather than as evidence for a mechanism.

The infant templates are averages. The same check placed their scalps << F.wr_qc_templates_offset_outside_otsu_mm_range >>~mm outside their own MRI head boundaries, whereas the adult's lies inside, so relative to that boundary both systems' sensors sit farther from a template's brain than from the adult's, which may lower the templates' $D$ and $\Delta$. The check also classed the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template as << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_verdict >>: its white surface fitted its T1 image best after a << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_white_best_shift_mm >>-mm shift (<< F.wr_qc_templates_other_white_best_shift_mm >>~mm in the other two templates), a possible misalignment or the weak contrast of an average image.

