\subsection{Noise model, validation and sensitivity analyses}\label{sec:noise}

Every array saw identical noise sources in one band of << F.g2_band_range_hz >>~Hz. Each array's oracle covariance was computed in closed form as
\begin{equation}
\symbf{C} = \symbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{n}} + \sigma^{2}\,\symbf{L}\symbf{A}\symbf{L}^{\top} + \symbf{E}\symbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{e}}\symbf{E}^{\top},
\label{eq:C}
\end{equation}
where $\symbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{n}}$ is the diagonal covariance of white sensor noise, $\symbf{L}$ holds the lead fields of << F.g2_n_background >> independent cortex-normal background dipoles on a << F.g2_bg_grid_mm >>-mm grid, $\symbf{A}$ is the diagonal matrix of their Voronoi-cell areas, and $\symbf{E}$ and $\symbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{e}}$ are the array's responses to << F.g2_room_terms >> external-field terms and their coefficient covariance. Each background dipole's moment variance is thus $\sigma^{2}$ times its area ($\sigma^{2}$ = << F.meth_bg_var_per_mm2_nam2 >>~nAm$^{2}$ per mm$^{2}$). Like the targets, the background omitted the cortex within << F.g2_source_skull_clearance_mm >>~mm of the inner skull.

Two quantities were fitted to the sample dataset: $\symbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{e}}$, to its empty-room recording, and the scalar $\sigma^{2}$, so that the median good gradiometer's modeled brain noise matched the measured one (a root-mean-square value of << F.meth_bg_calibration_grad_ft_cm >>~fT/cm). Apart from the declared sensor noise of both systems, everything else is a prediction. We analyzed four noise conditions: sensor noise only; sensor plus brain noise, the primary condition; sensor, brain and room noise; and the last with the room field's subspace removed from every array by one noise-weighted projection. This projected condition was named as the secondary condition before any simulation.

To validate the model, we compared its Neuromag covariance with the measured covariance of the same recording and recomputed Neuromag's detectability with the measured one (Section~S3). Because the OPM's covariance cannot be measured here, we computed the implied OPM/Neuromag ratio under five stated assumptions about how each system's real noise departs from the model (scenarios A--E; Section~\ref{sec:res-noise}). Each sensitivity analysis changed one element of the adult model and recalibrated $\sigma^{2}$. The cortex << F.rev_ns_joint_floor_mm >>--<< F.rev_ns_near_rule_mm >>~mm from the inner skull was added to the background, to the targets, or both. The OPM noise was colored (1/$f$ plus white noise, with corners at << F.rev_ns_col_corners_hz >>~Hz) under a matched filter that also whitens across frequency. Idealized cardiac and ocular far-field sources were added, and all of these changes were combined.

\subsection{Simulated interictal spikes}\label{sec:spikes}

The spike study ran the noise model in the time domain, in segments shared by all arrays, with the spike-wave complex of \citet{hunold2016} stretched to << F.wr_n_morphologies_words >> morphologies as the spike. Two matched-filter detectors read the data, both whitened with a Ledoit--Wolf covariance \citep{ledoit2004} estimated from << F.g4_null_baseline_minutes >>~min of null data rather than with the oracle covariance. The oracle detector knew the source and the time and tested at a per-trial false-positive probability of << F.g4_oracle_alpha >>. The practical detector knew neither: it scanned candidate field patterns on a << F.g4_dictionary_spacing_mm >>-mm grid that did not contain the true sources, with thresholds for a nominal << F.g4_operating_point_per_minute >> false event per minute frozen on independent null data. Both detectors are idealized, because they use the true forward model and the simulated waveform family. S50, the dipole strength for << F.g4_s50_level >> detection, was interpolated linearly in log strength between the tested strengths.

In the exploratory run, spikes were placed at << F.g4_n_locations >> locations per anatomy, stratified by orientation and by depth in << F.wr_n_depth_bands_words >> bands from << F.g4_band_10_20mm >> to << F.g4_band_45_70mm >>~mm (<< F.g4_locations_per_band >> locations each). Each location received << F.g4_n_focal_events_per_location >> focal events of << F.g4_strength_range >>~nAm and patch events, each in its own noise realization and identical for every array. In this and the confirmatory run, the adult sat at its measured head position, where its $D$ is << F.g3b_adult_centred_minus_top_d_abs >>~dB higher than at top contact (Table~\ref{tab:estimands}; Section~\ref{sec:res-heads}), and the smaller heads at top contact; every comparison was made within one anatomy.

The confirmatory design was fixed before the run in a configuration file of the public code repository; it was not registered externally. The endpoint compared the dense OPM array with Neuromag (<< F.g2_squid_channels >> channels) under the practical detector at a nominal << F.g4_operating_point_per_minute >> false event per minute, for focal spikes << F.g4_band_10_20mm >>~mm below the scalp in all << F.wr_n_anatomies_words >> anatomies. The threshold, set on << F.cf_null_calibration_minutes >>~min of calibration null data, rests on about << F.wr_confirm_calib_expected_events >> false events, a Poisson uncertainty of about << F.wr_confirm_calib_poisson_pct >>. The test was a two-sided sign-flip test on the per-location differences in detection counts, Holm-adjusted over the anatomies \citep{holm1979} at $\alpha$ = << F.meth_alpha >>, with the paired S50 ratio $r$ as the effect size, so that the OPM's << F.g4_s50_level >> detection strength is lower by $1 - 1/r$. The declared test estimated p by Monte Carlo beyond << F.meth_signflip_exact_max >> nonzero differences; we report exact p values computed afterwards from the stored differences, which give the same decisions in all << F.cf_exact_n_families >> test families.

The confirmatory run drew << F.meth_confirm_locs >> new locations per anatomy and << F.wr_confirm_replicates_words >> noise replicates per event, the first carrying the endpoint, and used separate null data for threshold fitting and for rate evaluation. The << F.meth_confirm_locs >> locations double the exploratory << F.wr_confirm_pilot_locs_per_band >> per band: << F.wr_confirm_pilot_runs_words >> pilot runs on test seeds in child A (locations favoring the OPM and Neuromag, << F.wr_confirm_pilot_locs >>; p = << F.wr_confirm_pilot_p >>), whose outputs were not kept, prompted the doubling but do not estimate power. Secondary analyses were a declared mismatched detector (off-template morphologies, candidate patterns from a single-compartment BEM and a shared coregistration error of << F.wr_confirm_coreg_shift_mm >>~mm and << F.wr_confirm_coreg_angle_deg >>°), the site-matched array, the oracle detector and rate-matched thresholds re-fitted on held-out null data. In the exploratory run, we localized detected spikes at << F.loc_n_locations >> locations by equivalent-current-dipole fits and by dynamic statistical parametric mapping (dSPM; \citealp{dale2000}) at the true peak sample, through a single-compartment BEM with a coregistration error of << F.loc_coreg_shift_mm >>~mm and << F.loc_coreg_angle_deg >>° (Section~S7).

\subsection{Statistical analysis}\label{sec:stats}

Neighboring targets are correlated, so intervals are << F.g2_ci_level_pct >> percentile bootstrap intervals over the adult's << F.g2_n_parcels >> atlas labels or over each smaller head's parcels (numbers of resamples in Table~S16). They describe spatial variation within one anatomy, not variation between subjects or head placements. We call a ratio established when its interval excludes 1, and a difference in dB when its interval excludes 0. In the spike study, the location was the unit of resampling and testing. The exploratory spike endpoint (chosen from << F.g4_family_per_opm_array >> comparisons per OPM array), the primary helmet placement and the regions of interest were named after the whole-cortex results were known, and the model was revised while results were known (Section~S8). Only the confirmatory run carries a pre-specified test.

