\subsection{Depth and brain regions}\label{sec:res-depth}

The dense lead was largest near the scalp (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}A): the ratio was << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_10_15_ratio >> << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_10_15_ci >> in the << F.g2_depth_bin_10_15_mm >>~mm bin, << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_25_30_ratio >> << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_25_30_ci >> at << F.g2_depth_bin_25_30_mm >>~mm and << F.g2_depth_dense_vs_combined_ib_35_60_range >> from the << F.g2_depth_bin_35_40_mm >> to the << F.g2_depth_bin_55_60_mm >>~mm bin, every interval above 1. The site-matched array had an equal-SNR depth even with sensor plus brain noise: its intervals lay above 1 down to the << F.rev_db_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci_above1_through_bin >>~mm bin and below 1 from << F.rev_db_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci_below1_from_bin >>~mm. With the room field projected out, the dense array's bin median fell below 1 from << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_proj_median_below1_from_bin >>~mm and the site-matched array's from << F.rev_db_matched_vs_combined_proj_median_below1_from_bin >>~mm, with intervals below 1 only at << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_proj_bins_ci_below1 >>~mm and from << F.rev_db_matched_vs_combined_proj_ci_below1_from_bin >>~mm, respectively.

For the dense array, the fall with depth was geometric. It was present with sensor noise alone (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}A, dotted) and in the peak field itself (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}B), and brain noise multiplied the ratio by a nearly constant factor in every bin (<< F.g2_brainmult_dense_vs_combined_range >>). On the cortex (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}C), the dense array was ahead almost everywhere, most of all on the lateral convexity.

By region (Fig.~\ref{fig:regions}), the dense lead was largest in the frontal lobe (<< F.g2_lobe_frontal_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_lobe_frontal_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>) and smallest in the cingulate (<< F.g2_lobe_cingulate_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_lobe_cingulate_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>) and the insula (<< F.g2_lobe_insula_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>). Of the regions chosen from the epilepsy literature, the precentral gyrus favored the dense array most (<< F.reg_precentral_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>) and the parahippocampal cortex least (<< F.reg_parahippocampal_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>), although << F.reg_dk_dense_vs_combined_ib_below_parahippocampal_count >> atlas parcels, mostly cingulate and medial, had smaller leads. The site-matched array trailed most on ventral and polar surfaces, such as the frontal pole (<< F.reg_dk_frontalpole_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>), the medial and lateral orbitofrontal cortex (<< F.reg_dk_medialorbitofrontal_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> and << F.reg_dk_lateralorbitofrontal_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>) and the temporal pole (<< F.reg_dk_temporalpole_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>), and, among the literature's regions, in the mesial temporal cortex (parahippocampal and entorhinal; << F.reg_mesial_temporal_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>).

These rankings are exploratory point estimates under the modeled noise. Under scenario D (Neuromag as measured, the OPM as modeled), the dense ratio became << F.wr_cov_s4_dense_lobe_cingulate_ratio >> in the cingulate, << F.wr_cov_s4_dense_lobe_parietal_ratio >> in the parietal, << F.wr_cov_s4_dense_lobe_frontal_ratio >> in the frontal and << F.wr_cov_s4_dense_lobe_temporal_ratio >> in the temporal lobe (point estimates). With the room field included, it fell below 1 from the << F.wr_cov_s4_dense_depth_first_below1_label >>~mm bin.

