\subsection{Helmet fit versus head size}\label{sec:res-fit}

The helmet fitted at the adult's gap was the main control (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}). In each scaled adult and template, it restored to Neuromag what the fixed helmet's gap and placement took away: this within-head contrast (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}D) was << F.rev_cgap_templates_scaled_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib_range >>~dB at the adult's gap and << F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_within_top_range >>~dB at the adult's top-contact gap. Of the extra advantage these heads showed in the fixed helmet (<< F.g3b_templates_scaled_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB), little remained. Fitted at the adult's top-contact gap, they kept << F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_fittedtop_vs_top_range >>~dB against the adult at top contact (<< F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_fittedtop_vs_top_n_ci_above0 >> intervals above zero). Fitted at the adult's gap, their $\Delta$ against the adult fitted the same way was << F.rev_cgap_templates_scaled_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB (point estimates; interval bounds << F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_fitted_delta_ci_bounds_range >>~dB). Every template interval included zero, and only the school-age scaled adult's lay just above it; the highest value was that of the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template, which the MRI check classed as << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_verdict >>. With the room field projected out, every smaller head's point estimate at the adult's gap lay below the adult's (<< F.rev_cgap_smaller_fitted_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta_range >>~dB; << F.wr_cgap_fitted_proj_delta_n_ci_includes_zero_words >> of the eight intervals including zero).

Set against the adult placed by the same rule in both helmets, the reduction from the fixed to the fitted helmet is the interaction, the extra that the fixed helmet gives a smaller head beyond what it gives the adult. It was << F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_interaction_range >>~dB in the scaled adults and templates at the adult's gap, every interval above zero (per head in Table~S6), and << F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_interaction_top_range >>~dB at the adult's top-contact gap. The interaction exceeds the heads' own within-head contrasts because the adult's contrast is negative, << F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib >>~dB: the fitted helmet holds the adult laterally centered, a looser pose than top contact. At the adult's top-contact gap, its contrast is << F.wr_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib >>~dB, the effect of the construction itself.

In the children, $\Delta$ at the adult's gap was negative in A and B (<< F.rev_cgap_child_a_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.rev_cgap_child_a_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >> and << F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >>~dB). Fitted at the adult's top-contact gap and set against the adult at top contact, however, it was not established (<< F.rev_cgap_child_a_fittedtop_vs_fixed_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.rev_cgap_child_a_fittedtop_vs_fixed_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >> and << F.rev_cgap_child_b_fittedtop_vs_fixed_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.rev_cgap_child_b_fittedtop_vs_fixed_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >>~dB; Section~S5), and their within-head contrasts were << F.rev_cgap_child_a_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib >> and << F.rev_cgap_child_b_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib >>~dB. In child C, whose two gaps nearly coincide (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}A), $\Delta$ was << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >>~dB, and its within-head contrast, << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib >> << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib_ci >>~dB, matched the effect of the construction itself (<< F.wr_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib >> << F.wr_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib_ci >>~dB in the adult at an equal median gap).

None of these quantities apportions the effects exactly, because the fitted helmet changes the gap distribution, the placement and the sensors' coverage together. In the fitted helmet and at equal OPM site count, $\Delta$ was << F.rev_cgap_smaller_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB (Section~S5). In the scaled adults and templates, their fewer OPM sites took about that much back, whereas in children A and B no such gain was established (<< F.rev_cgap_child_a_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.rev_cgap_child_a_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >> and << F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >>~dB). In this model, fitting the helmet to the adult's median gap thus removed most of the extra advantage that the scaled adults and templates showed in the fixed adult helmet, without isolating the median gap as the cause.

