\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

In a realistic adult head with explicit noise, a dense array of << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>-fT/√Hz OPMs detected cortical sources modestly better than the Neuromag system, whereas an array at Neuromag's own sites did not. The lead depended on the OPM noise floor and on how the comparison was made, and it was as large as the model's error for Neuromag's noise. In smaller heads, most of the extra advantage that the OPM gained in the fixed adult helmet disappeared when the helmet was fitted to each head at the adult's gap. With an idealized detector, the dense array detected weaker superficial spikes than Neuromag in all << F.wr_n_anatomies_words >> anatomies, without an established localization difference.

\subsection{Noise floor, array design and the noise model}\label{sec:disc-noise}

The spherical model of \citet{jas2026} places the OPM advantage within an equal-SNR depth set by an assumed noise ratio. With explicit noise, the picture changes in two ways. The dense array leads at every depth, and it shows an equal-SNR depth only after the room projection (by bin medians, with an interval below 1 only at << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_proj_bins_ci_below1 >>~mm) or with Neuromag's measured noise (scenario D, point estimates). The site-matched array, in contrast, keeps an equal-SNR depth even with sensor plus brain noise. Depth therefore sets the size of the dense lead, whereas its sign is set by the OPM noise floor and by the array: between << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_min >> and << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >>~fT/√Hz, or between the dense and the site-matched array, the comparison reverses.

The fall of the dense lead with depth is geometric. It appears without brain noise and in the peak field itself, and brain noise scales the ratio by a nearly constant factor at every depth. Brain noise turns the SQUID advantage into an OPM advantage because it raises the noise of a Neuromag magnetometer about << F.g2_noise_inflation_squid_mag_1dp >>-fold over its low sensor floor but that of an OPM only about << F.g2_noise_inflation_dense_1dp >>-fold (Section~S3), so the OPM's higher sensor noise matters less once brain noise dominates. The comparator and the metric then decide how a reported number shows the difference: a peak-channel SNR against magnetometers favors the OPM, one against the best of all channels favors Neuromag, and a channel-averaged SNR shows little difference.

How far the dense lead can be trusted depends on the OPM's real noise. The model predicted << F.rev_cov_brain_mag_amp_ratio >> of Neuromag's measured magnetometer brain-noise amplitude without being fitted to the magnetometers, but its error for Neuromag's detectability was as large as the dense lead. Depending on how the OPM shares that error, the five scenarios placed the dense ratio between << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s3_ratio >> and << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s1_ratio >>. The lead survived the omissions we could test, although together they lowered the noise level up to which it was established from << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_20 >> to << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz. The dense lead is therefore a prediction rather than a measurement, and a measured OPM noise covariance would test it directly.

The regional pattern is exploratory and rests on the modeled noise. Under that noise, the dense lead is largest on the frontal and central convexity and smallest in cingulate cortex, and the site-matched array trails on ventral and polar surfaces, including the mesial temporal cortex. With Neuromag's measured noise (scenario D, point estimates), the frontal lead falls to about 1, the cingulate and parietal lobes fall below 1, and the temporal lobe leads.

\subsection{Head size and helmet fit}\label{sec:disc-fit}

The sphere predicts a larger OPM benefit in smaller heads because a shell at a constant standoff leaves more of a small brain within the equal-SNR depth \citep{jas2026}. In the fixed adult helmet, every smaller head's point estimate of $\Delta$ was indeed positive. Fitting the helmet to each head at the adult's gap, however, removed most of this extra advantage in the scaled adults and templates (an interaction of << F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_interaction_range >>~dB, every interval above zero). What remained, << F.rev_cgap_scaled_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB in the scaled adults and << F.rev_cgap_templates_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB in the templates against the adult fitted the same way, or << F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_fittedtop_vs_top_range >>~dB against the adult at top contact, depends on the adult's reference placement. In this model, the extra advantage of smaller heads in the adult helmet thus comes mainly from how that helmet fits them, its gap, placement and coverage together, rather than from head size itself.

This conclusion does not rest on the templates. The two scaled adults keep the adult's anatomy, and their fixed-helmet extra of << F.g3b_school_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> and << F.g3b_size2yr_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >>~dB fell to << F.rev_cgap_school_fittedtop_vs_fixed_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> and << F.rev_cgap_size2yr_fittedtop_vs_fixed_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >>~dB against the adult at top contact when the helmet was fitted at the adult's top-contact gap. They also gave the lowest and the highest interactions of the scaled adults and templates (<< F.rev_cgap_school_fitted_combined_ib_interaction >> and << F.rev_cgap_size2yr_fitted_combined_ib_interaction >>~dB; templates, << F.wr_cgap_templates_interaction_range >>~dB). The fitted helmet changes the gap distribution, the placement and the coverage together, so it does not isolate the median gap as the cause. At equal OPM site count, $\Delta$ was << F.rev_cgap_smaller_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB, the quantity comparable to the sensor-noise-only result of \citet{zahran2022}, and in the scaled adults and templates the smaller heads' fewer OPM sites took this gain back. These controls do not show what produces either effect.

For pediatric MEG, these results suggest that much of the extra on-scalp advantage in small heads reflects the adult helmet's fit, so that a cryogenic helmet that fits the child's head would be expected to recover part of it. Our fitted helmet does not model such a system, which exists for young children \citep{tesan2010}, and how a child is positioned in an adult helmet matters as well \citep{gaetz2008}. The children's values are provisional: two of them fell below the adult fitted the same way, but not in the top-contact comparison.

\subsection{Simulated spikes and the clinical literature}\label{sec:disc-clinical}

In the confirmatory run, the dense array detected superficial simulated spikes at << F.cf_all_dense_vs_combined_practical_reduction_pct_range >> lower strength than Neuromag in all << F.wr_n_anatomies_words >> anatomies, and the ranking survived the declared detector mismatch. The S50 ratios were of the order of the adult's detectability ratio at these depths, so the detectability difference carried over to detection with an idealized detector. The run kept the fixed adult helmet and the primary noise model, so it does not show whether helmet fit drives a detection benefit in smaller heads, nor whether the benefit would survive the OPM noise of scenario D. No localization difference was established; dipole-fit errors were of the order of the coregistration error, which may have dominated the comparison.

The clinical reports and the model are compatible, but the model cannot identify which factor operated in any study (Table~\ref{tab:literature}). Higher or similar OPM SNR against SQUID magnetometers \citep[mixed in \citealp{feys2025}]{feys2022} corresponds to the model's peak-channel SNR against the magnetometers, which favors the OPM in every head (Section~S6). Equal or lower SNR against planar gradiometers, or against the nearest channels of few or noisier sensors \citep{hillebrand2023,schwartz2025}, corresponds to peak-channel SNR against the gradiometers or the best channel. The larger gains reported in school-aged children in an adult helmet \citep{feys2022} are compatible with the fixed helmet's fit, its gap and placement together (Sections~\ref{sec:res-heads} and \ref{sec:res-fit}).

The weaker OPM results for temporal and mesial temporal sources \citep{feys2024,feys2025,shen2026} have two analogues in the model: deep sources are weaker for every system, and the site-matched array trails on ventral and polar surfaces. The second analogue is fragile, because the model overstates Neuromag's detectability in the temporal lobe (measured over modeled, << F.rev_cov_det_lobe_temporal_corrected_ratio >>; Section~S3). With a channel-averaged metric, the site-matched array had a small but established lead (<< F.g2_meanpow_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_meanpow_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci >>) where \citet{matsubara2026} found no region with a higher OPM SNR, with brain noise only and in patients' anatomies. A cohort's report of no significant difference in detection \citep{ren2025} cannot adjudicate the model's predictions, whose endpoints differ (whole-cortex detectability and the detection of simulated superficial spikes).

By whole-cortex detectability against Neuromag's << F.g2_squid_channels >> channels, a single-axis array like the site-matched one (<< F.g2_matched_sites >> sites, about the channel count of the larger clinical arrays) showed no advantage at << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz and a SQUID advantage when noisier. Its small established advantages depended on the comparator, the metric, the covariance estimate or depth, within about << F.rev_db_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci_above1_to_mm >>~mm of the scalp, including simulated superficial spikes in << F.cf_matched_vs_combined_practical_n_holm_pass >> of << F.meth_n_anatomies >> anatomies. Clinical arrays reach their channel counts with dual- or triaxial sensors at fewer sites. The triaxial control at the site-matched sites (<< F.g2_triax_equal_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>) shows what extra axes can recover there, but the model does not determine whether an array of about 64 dual-axis sites would lie above or below the site-matched array. The modest whole-cortex advantage thus belongs to an array with more sites than any used in epilepsy so far, at a white noise within the clinical range.

