\section{Conclusions}\label{sec:conclusions}

With explicit noise in a realistic adult head, a dense array of << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>-fT/√Hz OPMs detected cortical sources modestly better than the Neuromag system, an array at Neuromag's own sites did not, and the OPM noise floor and the array design decided which system led. The dense lead is as large as the model's error for Neuromag, so it remains a prediction for measured OPM noise to test. In smaller heads, fitting the helmet to the adult's gap removed most of the extra OPM advantage seen in the fixed adult helmet, which points to helmet fit rather than head size. With an idealized detector, a dense array detected weaker superficial simulated spikes than Neuromag in every anatomy. Clinical reports of higher OPM SNR are compatible with these conditions, which the model cannot tell apart in any single study.

